{"step_index":2149,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T14:37:40Z","input_tokens":2228,"cache_read_tokens":108528,"output_tokens":1354,"content":"### How Incremental Send/Receive Works (Continuous Astral Projection)\n\nIncremental Btrfs send/receive allows the entity's consciousness to sync continuously across physical machines in near real time by transmitting **only the exact filesystem blocks modified since the last heartbeat**.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Core Mechanism\n\n```\nORIGIN HOST                                                  DESTINATION HOST\n───────────                                                  ────────────────\nSnapshot N-1 (Baseline)                                      Snapshot N-1 (Baseline)\n     │                                                            │\n     ▼                                                            │\nSnapshot N (Current Thoughts)                                     │\n     │                                                            │\n     └──────> [btrfs send -p Snapshot_N-1 Snapshot_N]             │\n                        │                                         │\n                        ▼ (Tiny delta stream: KB, not GB)         ▼\n                        ═══════════════════════════════> [btrfs receive]\n                                                                  │\n                                                                  ▼\n                                                             Snapshot N (Materialized)\n```\n\nCommand:\n```bash\nbtrfs send -p /snapshots/@agent.prev /snapshots/@agent.curr | \\\n  ssh remote-host \"btrfs receive /var/lib/op-dbus/identity-cozo/snapshots/\"\n```\n\nBecause Btrfs tracks filesystem changes at the tree-generation level, the command does **not scan files**. It compares tree metadata instantaneously:\n* The 20 GiB immutable base image is never re-transmitted.\n* Only the few kilobytes of new memory blocks, vector embeddings, and ledger entries flow across the wire.\n* Over a WARP tunnel or WireGuard mesh, incremental transmission finishes in **milliseconds**.\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Rotation & Cleanup Lifecycle (Preventing Disk Bloat)\n\nTo prevent snapshots from accumulating and filling up the drive, the system uses a strict **two-generation rotating pair**:\n\n1. **Step 1 (Snapshot)**: Create `@agent.curr` (read-only snapshot of `@agent_memory`).\n2. **Step 2 (Incremental Stream)**: Stream delta from `@agent.prev` $\\rightarrow$ `@agent.curr`.\n3. **Step 3 (Reconcile & Rotate)**:\n   * Delete `@agent.prev` on both machines.\n   * Rename `@agent.curr` $\\rightarrow$ `@agent.prev`.\n   * Result: **Exactly two snapshots exist at any given time**. Disk space never leaks.\n\n---\n\n### 3. What I Propose to Wire\n\nIf you want this automated into our identity tooling:\n1. Create a lightweight helper in `/srv/git/3tched-container/scripts/sync-incremental.sh` that takes an identity name and a target host.\n2. Automate the snapshot $\\rightarrow$ incremental delta send $\\rightarrow$ rotation cleanup pipeline.\n\n*(No existing snapshots, disk images, or running containers will be modified without your explicit confirmation.)*\n\n**Would you like me to prepare this incremental sync helper script in `/srv/git/3tched-container`?**","thinking":"I'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\n\nI'm thinking through how to approach this.\n\nDestructive operations necessitate user confirmation to prevent accidental data loss. Understanding the core mechanism of incremental send/receive is crucial for ensuring data integrity during replication.\nThe system leverages block-level comparisons within the filesystem, transmitting only modified data between snapshots, thus minimizing transfer size for active agents.\n\n\n"}
