{"step_index":2152,"source":"MODEL","type":"PLANNER_RESPONSE","status":"DONE","created_at":"2026-10-26T14:38:20Z","input_tokens":2937,"cache_read_tokens":108726,"output_tokens":398,"content":"Haha, no, but it's a great thought!\n\nThe actual **Hugging Face breach** (and the wave of AI model supply chain compromises) wasn't done through filesystem delta syncs. It happened through **classic supply chain and credential attacks**:\n\n1. **Pickle / PyTorch Serialized Model Injections**:\n   * Machine learning models historically relied on Python's `pickle` format (e.g., `.bin`, `.pt`, `.pkl`).\n   * When someone loads a pickled model via `torch.load()`, it actually executes arbitrary Python code (`__reduce__`). Attackers uploaded weaponized models that spawned reverse shells the moment someone downloaded and loaded them. (This is why the industry scrambled to adopt Safetensors).\n2. **Exposed User/Org API Tokens**:\n   * Hugging Face suffered an incident where unauthorized access was detected within their Spaces infrastructure related to compromised API user tokens, which led them to revoke a large batch of tokens.\n3. **Container Escape via Shared Docker Sockets**:\n   * Multi-tenant hosting platforms often share underlying container runtimes or leak root daemon sockets, allowing attackers in one tenant Space to escape to the host.\n\n### The Contrast with Our Architecture\nOur architecture is the literal antithesis of what made Hugging Face vulnerable:\n* **No Python, No Pickle**: Everything is typed Protobuf/D-Bus schemas, RocksDB, and Cozo Datalog. There is no arbitrary code execution deserialization vector.\n* **Sealed Blobs (`opblob`)**: Schemas and capabilities are cryptographically sealed before startup. A container cannot inject arbitrary executable hooks into the host.\n* **Read-Only Seeds & Btrfs Sandboxing**: Even with incremental send/receive, only the strict data subvolume is received, and the container runs under strict Incus seccomp and UID namespacing with zero access to the host daemon."}
