e sparse partition (`p1`) and runs `device add`:\n   * Btrfs generates a **brand-new, unique filesystem UUID** for that container's active filesystem.\n   * To the container OS and processes, the root filesystem looks like a normal, private, read-write drive.\n   * **Every new write, file change, or log is redirected to the private sprout**.\n   * The shared seed is never touched.\n3. **Upgradability Without Duplication**:\n   When the platform upgrades, the host delivers the new layer via Btrfs send/receive or an updated lower layer. Thousands of containers instantly inherit the new binaries without copying 1,000 full operating systems.\n\n---\n\n### What Needs to Be Verified for Complete Safety\n\nTo guarantee this runs smoothly and never causes the uncoordinated collision you experienced before:\n\n1. **Verify the Seed Flag**:\n   Confirm that the shared image `/var/lib/incus/storage-pools/3tched/3tched-seed.img1` is strictly read-only and flagged as an immutable seed device (`btrfstune -S 1`).\n2. **Verify Sprout UUID Allocation**:\n   Confirm that when `launch-identity.sh` adds the sprout device inside the container, the new filesystem UUID is completely isolated from the host.\n\n---\n\n### Confirmation Request\n\nBefore we touch anything or prepare to launch any identity, I will run a read-only check to verify the seed device flags and sprout configuration.\n\n**Do you confirm running this read-only seed and sprout verification?**","thinking":"Confirmed understanding of the Btrfs seed/sprout illusion within a container environment. The intent is to describe the interaction of read-only rootfs sharing among multiple containers, while individual containers write to private leaf nodes. Because the operation is potentially destructive, user confirmation is crucial before detailing and enacting these steps.\n\n\n"}
